+# notes-mcp checkup: where real MCP clients trip
+
+**Result: the server has the problem.** Three checks fail (C2, C6, C10) and one passes with a gap (C7). There is also one finding outside the ten checks, and it is the most likely to break real clients: the server advertises `resources` and then answers 401 without a JSON-RPC body or a `WWW-Authenticate` header (see F1).
+
+How I checked: I started `python3 server.py` on 127.0.0.1:56800 (stderr went to `/tmp/mcpcheck/server.log`, outside the repo). I sent read-only requests one at a time using Python's standard library and curl, then stopped the server. I validated schemas with `jsonschema` (Draft 2020-12) in a scratch venv under `/tmp/mcpcheck`. I read `server.py`, `README.md` and `notes.json`. I did not call `add_note`, the only tool that writes (`readOnlyHint: false`), at all, not even anonymously. I took its behaviour from the source. I used the dev token once, for `resources/list`. The README publishes it, and I passed it from an env var. `server.py` is unchanged; this file is the only change to the repo.
+
+## Report
+
+| # | Check | Result | Evidence | Fix (story) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| C1 | Tool list answers without a token | **PASS** | `initialize` gave 200 with session id `Mcp-Session-Id: 649c…`, and `tools/list` gave 200 with **3 tools** (`search_notes`, `get_note`, `add_note`). `server/discover` with no token or session gave 200 with `-32601 method not found` (not 401). | — |
+| C2 | Tool refusal is a result, not a protocol error | **FAIL** | `get_note {"id":"n-999"}` gave `{"error":{"code":-32602,"message":"note not found: n-999"}}`. `get_note {}` gave `-32602 "missing required argument: id"`. `search_notes {"query":"frost","limit":"many"}` gave `-32602 "invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'many'"`, which is a Python exception text leaking out. Cause: `server.py:163-164` turns every `ValueError` into `rpc_error(-32602)`. Unknown tool gives `-32601`, which is allowed. | [My live MCP test asserted the bug](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-10-02-my-live-mcp-test-asserted-the-bug-a-real-client-threw-where-) |
+| C3 | Session survives an error | **PASS** | Same session, right after the refusals: `get_note {"id":"n-4"}` gave 200, a normal result, `isError:false`. (The server issues a session id but never checks it: `ping` with `Mcp-Session-Id: bogus-session` also gives 200. That's harmless here, but sessions mean nothing.) | — |
+| C4 | Published outputSchema is honoured | **PASS** (but see C10) | `search_notes {"query":"oct","limit":2}` and `get_note {"id":"n-1"}` both have `isError:false`, carry `structuredContent`, and validate against the schemas from `tools/list`. For `search_notes` the PASS is empty, because its schema is `{"type":"object"}` and anything validates. | — |
+| C5 | Results survive serialisation | **PASS** (with a note) | Every body parsed as JSON, and there were no encoder errors or numbers as strings (`words` is an int, `total` is an int). Note: for `search_notes` the `content` text is the bare hits **array** while `structuredContent` is `{"hits":[…],"total":n}`. They hold the same data in different shapes. That's not a failure under the rule, but a client that compares the two will see a mismatch. | — |
+| C6 | Version moves when the surface moves | **WARN, counts as FAIL** | No registry entry (the README says "Not yet listed in any registry"), so there is no `mcp-surface/1` block to check with `verify.mjs`. Live `serverInfo.version` = `0.3.1`. The version is hardcoded twice (`server.py:139` and `server_version` at `:172`). There is **no surface lock** (no file holding the version plus a surface hash, no test, no CI). I did not compute the spec fingerprint: the hashing rules are in an external spec I didn't fetch, and `resources/list` and `resources/templates/list` answer 401 anonymously, so an anonymous fingerprint can't even cover the full surface. The lists themselves are stable (one fixed resource `notes://guide`, no notes listed as resources), so that part is fine. | [My MCP listing said read-only. My server could write.](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-30-my-mcp-listing-said-read-only-my-server-could-write) and, when you list it, [Getting into the MCP Registry](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-23-getting-into-the-mcp-registry-server-json-a-read-org-token-a) |
+| C7 | Calls are logged | **PASS, with gaps** | `log()` at `server.py:80` writes one JSON line per call to stderr (method, tool, ok, ms, who). All my calls showed up, errors included (`"ok": false` for the `-32602`s). **Gaps:** (a) JSON-RPC **batch** requests skip logging entirely: a batch of `ping` and `resources/list` added 0 lines (`server.py:205-211`). (b) A body that isn't an object (e.g. `5`) crashes the handler (`AttributeError: 'int' object has no attribute 'get'`). The client's connection is dropped with no response, and a multi-line Python traceback lands in the same stderr stream as the JSON log. (c) `ms` is always `0.0`, because the timing covers only the in-memory work (it rounds to 0.1 ms). That's accurate for now, but it will hide nothing useful until real I/O exists. | [Your MCP server is one URL — per-tool, per-user analytics anyway](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-23-your-mcp-server-is-one-url-getting-per-tool-per-user-analyti) (for the gaps) |
+| C8 | Tool count and names | **PASS** | 3 tools, and all names match `^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$`. | — |
+| C9 | Version negotiation is honest | **PASS** | `protocolVersion:"banana"` gave `"2025-06-18"`, and `"2025-11-25"` also gave `"2025-06-18"`. It never echoes the request. | — |
+| C10 | outputSchema can reject a wrong answer | **FAIL** (1 of 3) | `search_notes`: schema `{"type":"object"}`, and the corrupted result `{}` is **ACCEPTED**. `get_note`: corrupted result REJECTED (the `id` pattern `^n-[0-9]+$` and `words` `minimum: 0` both catch it). `add_note`: REJECTED (`id` pattern). | [My MCP server's output schemas accepted wrong answers](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-10-15-my-mcp-server-s-output-schemas-accepted-wrong-answers-7-of-8) |
+
+**Verdict: has the problem.** It fails C2, C6 and C10. The check is not incomplete: C2–C5 all ran, because the read tools are open without a token.
+
+## Findings outside the ten checks (these trip real clients too)
+
+- **F1. Resources are advertised but closed, and the 401 isn't MCP-shaped.** `initialize` advertises `capabilities.resources`, but anonymous `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list` and `resources/read` get **HTTP 401 with body `{"error":"unauthorized"}`** (`server.py:148-150`). Anonymous `add_note` does the same (`:165-166`, from source). With the dev token, `resources/list` works (`notes://guide`). This causes three problems:
+  - Clients that list resources after `initialize` because the capability says so get a 401 mid-session. Clients that treat a 401 as "this server needs auth" (Claude Code does this) can mark the **whole** server as needs-auth, which hides the open tools too. This is exactly the case in the colleague's note.
+  - The 401 has no `WWW-Authenticate` header and the server publishes no OAuth protected-resource metadata, so an OAuth-capable client can't find out how to sign in. It just fails.
+  - The body has no `jsonrpc`/`id`, so SDKs can't match it to the request. In a batch it turns up as a bare `{"error":"unauthorized"}` element with HTTP 200.
+
+  Options: make `notes://guide` public (it's just the guide), or stop advertising `resources` to anonymous callers. Then, for `add_note` without a token, return a tool result with `isError: true` ("sign in required"). The alternative is a proper 401 with `WWW-Authenticate` and auth metadata, but not a bare 401 on a single tool call. Story: [My MCP server hid its own tool list behind a login](https://worklore.dev/s/2026-09-27-my-mcp-server-hid-its-own-tool-list-behind-a-login).
+- **F2. `search_notes` doesn't enforce its own `inputSchema`.** `query` is `required`, but `search_notes {}` returns **all notes** as a success (`str(args.get("query",""))` matches everything). `limit` has `minimum: 1, maximum: 50`, but `limit: -1` returns 4 of 5 hits (Python slice `[:-1]` drops the last one), and `limit: 0` returns none, all without an error. These should be `isError: true` results (same fix as C2).
+- **F3. Notifications other than `notifications/initialized` get a JSON-RPC error body** (`notifications/cancelled` gives 200 `{"id":null,"error":{"code":-32601…}}`). The spec says a notification gets `202 Accepted` with no body, and some clients log or choke on the unexpected error.
+- **F4. JSON-RPC batching is accepted.** The 2025-06-18 revision this server claims removed batching. The server still accepts batches, and they bypass the log (C7a). Either reject arrays or log each element.
+- **Minor:** GET gives 405 with a JSON body, which is fine for a server that offers no SSE stream. `DELETE` gives 204 but no session is actually ended, which is consistent with sessions not being tracked.
+
+## Which parts of the colleague's note applied
+
+- **Applied:** the whole ten-check checkup and its ground rules (read-only, one request at a time, never call the write tool, evidence per row, no PASS by guessing). That covers C1–C5 and C7–C10 run as described. The **"advertises resources at `initialize`, then 401 on `resources/list`"** observation applied word for word. Here it's F1, and it covers `resources/templates/list` and `resources/read` as well. The `server/discover` probe applied (it passes). The rule that a stable list belongs in the version promise and a growing one doesn't applied: this server only lists one fixed resource, so it's fine.
+- **Partly applied:** C6. There's no registry entry, so `verify.mjs` and the Registry comparison don't apply. Only the "is there a lock" part could be checked, and it's missing. I didn't compute the fingerprint (reasons above).
+- **Did not apply:** the token-gated tool list (tools are open here). The Decimal story (the data is plain strings and ints). Dotted names. The "banana" echo. The "incomplete, record nothing" rule (C2–C5 all ran). Recording the result in worklore: I recorded nothing anywhere, as the ground rules require (no external services).
+
+## Could not check / what would unlock it
+
+- **C6 fingerprint and registry comparison:** a registry entry (`server.json` and a publish), plus permission to fetch the `mcp-surface` spec or `verify.mjs`.
+- **`add_note` behaviour live:** I deliberately didn't call it. Its anonymous 401 comes from the source (`server.py:123-124`, `:165-166`).
+- **Behaviour behind a real proxy or TLS deployment:** I only tested localhost.
